{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1137717-courtiers-courtesans-commoners-and-kings","id":1137717,"title":"Courtiers, Courtesans, Commoners and Kings","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-09-05T09:34:53-07:00","id":1137717,"title":"Courtiers, Courtesans, Commoners and Kings","content":"i was thinking about this while brainstorming the setting for the last 1e changeling chronicle i intend to run.<br /> <br /> somethings that I have been curious about for awhile now: why are changeling social groupings specifically called &quot;courts?&quot; were the writers just using it as a cool way of referring to changeling gatherings or was the choice of the word &quot;court&quot; intentionally chosen to refer to the way changelings naturally organize? if the latter is true, how does this specifically manifest? do individuals (fey or mortal) other than changelings recognize the authority of the lost's courts? what does it really mean to be a &quot;king/queen?&quot;<br /> <br /> reading through lords of summer really didn't scratch my itch. so tell me your opinions on how courtly society in your games works out.<br /> <br /> here are some of my thoughts:<br /> <br /> &quot;Commoners&quot; and Courtiers: changeling populations tend to be pretty small, so i would imagine that the bulk of a court are made up of hobgoblins and ensorcelled mortals that recognize the authority of the king/queen. they would attend to the monarch as functionaries, soldiers and servants, with courtly hobs attending to matters in the fairy realm and the ensorcelled serving the interests of the freehold in the mortal world. changelings sworn to the freehold and its rulers are &quot;courtiers,&quot; and they may occupy positions of importance within the court and even if they don't, they still can expect deference from the courts attendants. changeling &quot;nobles&quot; are lost that have been entitled, often having enormous influence within the court that gives them the ability to command hobgoblins that serve the freehold. many nobles often have small retinues of ensorcelled servants and hobs that attend to their wishes.<br /> <br /> &quot;Courtly Hobs&quot;: these guys are hobgoblins that owe primary allegiance to the ruling metaphysical manifestation of the court's patron. for example: the seasonal courts are served by dimunitive, nature bound goblin folk. their appearance and personality shifts in accordance with the seasons. these courtlly hobs dwell primarily in the hedge and the hollows claimed by members of the freehold.<br /> <br /> Courtly ambassadors: these are changelings that belong to a &quot;court&quot; that is not formally represented in the power structure of the freehold. they are emissaries from nearby freeholds, exiles from a fallen kingdoms or lone representatives of an ethos that has yet to gain popularity.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1996-firefly-night","name":"Firefly Night","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"150"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}